course: history
lesson: 32
title: Votes for women
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Two hundred years ago in most countries only men who owned property could vote, and no women anywhere could vote in national elections, as if they could not decide important things.
explain: Imagine your class voting on the school trip, but only the boys with a bike get a say: that was how elections worked, and many women and men set out to change it.
explain: Suffragists campaigned peacefully with petitions and marches, while the suffragettes in Britain chose bolder deeds, chaining themselves to railings and going to prison, so that nobody could ignore the question.
explain: New Zealand was first in 1893; others followed over many decades, and each group that won the vote, workers, women, Black citizens and young people, had to fight for it.
fact: Two hundred years ago in most countries only men with property could vote, and women could not vote in national elections anywhere.
fact: In 1893 New Zealand became the first self-governing country where women could vote in national elections, after a huge petition led by Kate Sheppard.
fact: Suffragists campaigned for votes for women with petitions, meetings and peaceful marches, and Millicent Fawcett led them in Britain.
fact: The British suffragettes, led by Emmeline Pankhurst, used bolder actions such as chaining themselves to railings, breaking windows and going on hunger strike in prison.
fact: Some people disliked the suffragettes' methods, but their actions made the question impossible to ignore.
fact: The suffragettes' motto was Deeds not words, and their colours were purple, white and green.
fact: During the First World War women worked in factories, drove ambulances and ran farms, and many people saw that women could do any job.
fact: In 1918 many British women over 30 won the vote, and in 1928 all women over 21 could vote, the same as men.
fact: In the USA women campaigned for decades, led by Susan B. Anthony and Elizabeth Cady Stanton, and in 1920 the 19th Amendment gave women the vote.
fact: Many Black women in the American South were still stopped from voting until the 1960s.
fact: Women could vote in Finland from 1906, in Norway from 1913, in France only from 1944, and in Switzerland's national elections only from 1971.
fact: In 1960 Sirimavo Bandaranaike of Sri Lanka became the world's first woman prime minister, and today women lead countries all over the world.
fact: Voting lets people choose who makes the laws they must live by.
exercise: Who could vote in most countries two hundred years ago? | Only men who owned property | Everyone over ten years of age | Only women | Only children
exercise: Which was the first self-governing country to give women the vote in national elections? | New Zealand | Great Britain | The United States | The French Republic
exercise: When did New Zealand women win the vote? | 1893 | 1066 | 1971 | 2001
exercise: Who led the big petition in New Zealand? | Kate Sheppard | Emmeline Pankhurst | Marie Curie of Poland | Queen Elizabeth I
exercise: How did suffragists campaign? | With petitions, meetings and peaceful marches | With armies, cannons and a march on the palace | By sailing to America | By printing fake money
exercise: Who led the suffragists in Britain? | Millicent Fawcett | Queen Victoria herself | Florence Nightingale | Jane Austen the writer
exercise: Who led the suffragettes? | Emmeline Pankhurst | Kate Sheppard of New Zealand | Rosa Parks | Joan of Arc
exercise: What bold actions did suffragettes take? | Chaining themselves to railings and going on hunger strike | Starting the Hundred Years' War again with the help of the French king | Building castles | Sailing round the world
exercise: What was the suffragettes' motto? | Deeds not words | Liberty or death | Keep calm and carry on | One small step for man
exercise: What were the suffragettes' colours? | Purple, white and green | Red, white and blue | Black and yellow | Orange, pink and sky blue
exercise: What jobs did many women do in the First World War? | Worked in factories, drove ambulances and ran farms | Nothing, they stayed at home and waited for the war to end | Only knitting | Flew to the Moon
exercise: When could British women over 21 vote, like men? | 1928 | 1893 | 1066 | 2018
exercise: What gave American women the vote in 1920? | The 19th Amendment | Magna Carta | The Treaty of Waitangi | The Declaration of Independence
exercise: Which European country gave women the national vote only in 1971? | Switzerland | Finland | Norway and Sweden | Britain
exercise: Who was the world's first woman prime minister? | Sirimavo Bandaranaike of Sri Lanka | Margaret Thatcher of the United Kingdom | Kate Sheppard of New Zealand | Elizabeth I of England
exercise: Why does voting matter? | It lets people choose who makes the laws | It gives everyone free money every single month | It makes the weather better | It is only a game
test: sis says women always had the vote it's just old movies making stuff up | No, they had to campaign for it, and it took until 1893 even for the first country | Yes, they always voted | Yes, since the Romans | No, women still cannot vote anywhere
test: Two groups wanted the same thing: one marched peacefully, one broke windows. Which was which? | Suffragists marched peacefully; suffragettes used bolder deeds | Suffragettes marched peacefully; suffragists broke windows | Both only wrote letters | Neither did anything
test: Why did the First World War change many people's minds about women voting? | Women did all kinds of jobs and showed they could do anything | Women won the war alone | Men stopped voting | The war was fought over voting, and women were the generals on both sides
test: A quiz says France was the first country with votes for women. Correct it. | New Zealand was first, in 1893; France waited until 1944 | France was first in 1066 | Switzerland was first in 1971 | It was the USA in 1776
test: In 1920 American women won the vote. Who still often found it hard to vote after that? | Many Black women in the South, until the 1960s | Nobody at all | Only women in New York and the big cities of the north | Only men
test: You see purple, white and green ribbons at a women's rights march. Why those colours? | They were the suffragettes' colours | They are the colours of the Union Jack | They are a football club's colours | They were chosen in 2020
test: What would happen in a country where only half the people could vote? | Laws would be made without the other half having a say | Laws would be fairer for everyone, since men always know best | Nothing would change at all | Nobody would make any laws
test: ugh voting's boring why'd they fight so hard for it | It lets you choose who makes the laws you have to live by | They had nothing else to do | It came with a free prize, like a toy, for everyone who voted | It is only for presidents
test: Your friend says Margaret Thatcher was the first woman prime minister in the world. What is right? | Sirimavo Bandaranaike of Sri Lanka was first, in 1960 | Thatcher was first, in 1900 | Kate Sheppard was first | Queen Victoria was first
test: In 1918, a British woman of 25 and her mum of 50 go to vote. Who could vote? | Only the mum, as many women over 30 could vote | Only the 25-year-old, because she was younger and could read | Both of them | Neither of them
exam: Identify the first self-governing nation to extend the franchise to women in national elections. | New Zealand | The United Kingdom | The United States | The Swiss Confederation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the methods of the suffragettes? | They employed direct actions including chaining themselves to railings | They confined themselves solely to petitions | They took no action whatsoever | They campaigned exclusively in New Zealand
exam: Ascertain the year in which women in the United Kingdom attained the franchise upon equal terms with men. | Nineteen hundred and twenty-eight | Eighteen hundred and ninety-three | Nineteen hundred and seventy-one | Nineteen hundred and forty-four
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the first woman to hold office as prime minister. | Sirimavo Bandaranaike of Sri Lanka, in nineteen hundred and sixty | Margaret Thatcher of Britain, in nineteen hundred and sixty | Emmeline Pankhurst of Britain, in nineteen hundred and eighteen | Kate Sheppard of New Zealand, in eighteen hundred and ninety-three
